econd: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, anorexia, ascites\n\nCushing's Syndrome (Hypercortisolism) vs Somatostatinoma (Delta Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormality of the thyroid gland, anorexia, ascites, chronic fatigue\n\nVIPoma (Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide-Producing Tumor) vs Somatostatinoma (Delta Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal gastrointestinal motility, benign gastrointestinal tract tumors, dehydration, elevated calcitonin, erythema\n  Favours second: gallbladder dysfunction, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, intestinal obstruction, lack of bowel sounds, neoplasm of the small intestine\n\nNOT typically seen in VIPoma (Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide-Producing Tumor): hematochezia\n\nRelated to Insulin Resistance Syndromes: diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\nRelated to Glucagonoma Syndrome (Alpha Cell Tumor): pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor\n\nRelated to Cushing's Syndrome (Hypercortisolism): acth-dependent cushing syndrome, acth-independent cushing syndrome, adrenal gland disease\n\nRelated to VIPoma (Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide-Producing Tumor): pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, pancreatic vasoactive intestinal peptide producing tumor, small intestinal vasoactive intestinal peptide producing tumor\n\nRelated to Somatostatinoma (Delta Cell Tumor): carcinoid tumor (disease), pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: Pathology_Robbins"}
